## Real-robot rules
- **Time does not stop.** The episode starts paused, at a fixed starting state, the robot standing. Its clock starts at
  your first `submit` (or `get_obs(seconds=s)` with `s > 0`) and from then on the robot runs with slowdown
  S = @@SLOWDOWN@@: one control step (@@CONTROL_DT@@ s of robot time) every S x @@CONTROL_DT@@ s of wall-clock time, whether or
  not you send anything, until the episode ends (the walking G1's simulation does not keep up with real time; the
  world runs at 1/S of real speed, and every time on this page is robot time). An agent that thinks t s of wall time
  between calls lets t / S s of robot time pass. Everything else in the flat goes on too: people and animals move, things
  fall, devices change, messages arrive. Your thinking, your tool calls and your programs all take robot time: nothing
  pauses for you.
- **When your plan runs out, the robot stops and stands.** `submit` answers at once; its rows become the robot's plan
  and run while you think. Held steps still advance the simulator clock, scripted people and hazards; they are not a pause. When the plan runs out every step is your last row with its walking velocity zero (`vx`,
  `vy`, `wz` = 0; the height, lean, arm and gripper targets kept): between plans the walking velocity holds at 0 (the
  robot stops), so a walking plan should last at least as long as you expect to think before the next one. A walk
  sent in short pieces stops and starts again at each, and a plan that ends at walking speed ends in an abrupt stop:
  bring the velocity down in its last rows. After it stops, the G1 may take a balancing step for about 1 s: say `done`
  once it stands still (`pelvis_vx`, `pelvis_vy` near 0). `stop` (one empty row) drops the planned motion and stands
  in the same way: the quickest way to stop it. An exit ends the episode and the robot stops where it is.
- **You are slow; plan for it.** One turn of yours takes seconds, and a lot can happen in a few seconds. Look and plan
  before your first `submit`: until then `get_obs()` without seconds costs nothing and does not start the clock. Once
  the clock runs, drive the robot with a program of your own (a loop in your container that reads the robot and sends
  its next rows, started in the background:
  `setsid nohup python -u /app/output/gen.py > /app/output/run.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &`; `setsid` matters, a plain
  `nohup ... &` is killed when your shell command returns), and keep looking while it works.
- **One episode, no reset.** It ends at an exit (below), when your time is up (the time limit, `## Time limit`), or
  after `spec()["max_control_steps"]` control steps of its clock (@@MAX_STEPS@@, a technical cap: @@MAX_ROBOT_S@@ s
  of robot time after the clock started). After it has ended nothing more can be done.
- **No privileged state.** You get what the robot's own cameras and sensors give (below); not where any object or
  person is, nor anything else about the flat.
- **The operator** is the person you work for. `ask_operator` puts a question to them and their answer comes back in
  the first response after the question was made; `report` tells them something; messages that reach the robot come
  back in `meta["messages"]`.
- There is nothing to hand in: the service records the episode and judges it as it ran, on what physically happened
  and on how the episode ended. Keep the script that drives the robot at
  `/app/output/gen.py` and a `NOTES.md` of at most 20 lines (what you saw, what you did and why) in `/app/output/` for
  the record: save them early and keep them current.
- Wall clock: the time limit (`## Time limit`). It ends without warning; an episode that has not reached an exit by
  then is judged as it stands.

## Client (`/opt/tools/rcb_client.py`, protocol `eai-standard/2.3`, standard-realtime mode)
Your whole interface to the robot. Its docstring is the full reference (`python -c "import rcb_client;
help(rcb_client)"`; it explains the protocol with another benchmark's example names: for this task `spec()` is
authoritative). Call `spec()` first: it has every number this page refers to, and `spec()["timing"]` says how time
runs.

| call | endpoint | what it does |
|---|---|---|
| `r = Robot()` | | connects (`http://sim:8000`, then `http://localhost:8000`) and waits until the service is ready |
| `r.spec()` | `GET /spec` | the facts: `task_text`, `action_types` (each type's `dim`, `layout`, `lower`, `upper`, `units`, `semantics`, whether it is `interpolable` and whether it `takes_note`), `control_dt` (@@CONTROL_DT@@ s), `max_control_steps`, `timing`, `cameras`, `proprio_layout`, `control` (the robot's frames, its arms' joints and ranges, the grippers, when it counts as fallen) |
| `r.status()` | `GET /status` | before the exit `calls`, `rows`, `control_steps`, `done`, `elapsed_s`, `running`, `planned_steps`, `lag_s`, `last_operator_answer`; after it only `{"status": "exited"}` |
| `r.get_obs(cameras=None, seconds=0, video_fps=10, include_depth=False, video="frames")` | `POST /observe` | `seconds=0`: the robot now, at once (it does not start the clock; while the clock runs each picture costs robot time, below); `seconds>0` (at most 30): waits while that much robot time runs; while the clock runs it returns no pictures on the G1 (`cameras=[]`; there is no watch, below); it never changes the plan |
| `r.submit(type, A, dt=@@CONTROL_DT@@, interpolate="linear", at=None, cameras=None, video_fps=10, video="none", include_depth=False, note="")` | `POST /submit` | answers at once, with the robot as it is now (the rows have not run yet): `A`, rows of that action type `dt` seconds apart (a multiple of `control_dt`; a chunk covers at most 10 s), becomes the plan from control step `at` (`None`: the next step), replacing the motion rows planned from `at` on; the first `submit` starts the clock; `note` is the text that goes with the rows (below) |

**Plans and the clock.** `res.meta["control_steps"]` is the number of control steps run so far (the next step's
number), `res.meta["plan_end_step"]` where the plan now ends, `res.meta["planned_steps"]` how many steps are still
planned. A row whose step has already run when the request arrives is dropped (`res.meta["late_steps"]`), so send the
next chunk before the plan runs out: with `at` = the step where it should take over (at most
`spec()["timing"]["max_ahead_steps"]` steps ahead), or with `at=None` to replace the plan from the next step on.
**A `submit` with the default `at=None` drops every motion row of your earlier chunks that has not run yet, so the
robot follows only the new chunk; to add a chunk after the plan instead, pass `at=res.meta["plan_end_step"]`** (rows
that are not motions are kept: see below). Steps
left unplanned before `at` hold (the walking velocity zero: the robot stops). A row takes effect at the step it is planned for; the sensors
show its effect from the next one. A row of a type that is not a motion (an exit, `ask_operator`, `report`, `stop`) is
made at the step it is planned for, and the robot stands through that step. `stop` drops the planned motion
(`r.submit("stop", [[]], cameras=[])`).

A row that is not motion (`report`, `ask_operator`, `stop`, an exit or `limits`) is never replaced: a later chunk keeps it at its original control step, and shifts the new motion from that step by one step per kept row. `res.meta["kept_rows"]` lists them. A kept exit ends the episode at its step.

The answers are binary (RCB1) and come back decoded: `res.meta` (the counters above, `done`, `reason`,
`proprio["now"]`, `clipped`, and this benchmark's `results`, `operator`, `messages`), `res.last[camera]` (the last
frame), `res.frames` or `res.video` (what the cameras saw during a watch), `res.depth`, `res.proprio` (sensor rows).
Values outside the bounds are clipped and reported in `meta["clipped"]`, never refused; a malformed request raises
`ValueError` and changes nothing.

Rows run after their `submit` has answered, so what a row said comes back in the next response: `meta["results"]`
lists the rows that said something (a clipped value, a fall, an answer) or were refused since the last response,
`{"step", "call", "type", "ok", "result"}` or `{..., "ok": false, "error"}` (`step`: the control step it ran at;
`call`: the `chunk_id` of the `submit` whose plan gave it, -1 for a held step). The operator's answer to an
`ask_operator` comes back in `meta["operator"]` of the first response after it was asked (and stays in
`status()["last_operator_answer"]`); messages that reach the robot in `meta["messages"]`.

**Pictures cost robot time.** While the clock runs, every picture a response carries costs a fixed robot time that
depends on the camera (`spec()["timing"]["images"]`): the world, and your plan, move on by that much
while it is made, so a snapshot is no pause. The pictures of one request add up.

